Link the CPU interrupt controller into the service plane only

registerCPUInterruptController() hands a controller that nothing ever
attached to registerService(), which drops it as a non member.  The fix has to
put it in the plane, but attaching it to the platform expert also gives it a
provider, and IOService::_adjustBusy walks provider edges while the caller
still holds its own arbitration lock: same thread, two locks, one cycle.  The
panel then repeats

    nio: SM locked IOCPUInterruptController
    nio: SM b4busy cfg=1
    nio: SM rec c=2

for ever, which is exactly what the recursive-acquisition line is reporting.

attachToParent(getRegistryRoot(), gIOServicePlane) links it into the service
plane and nothing else.  registerService() only asks that the chain reach the
root; no provider means no busy propagation through this object.

AT prints on the way in, AT cpuic fail if the registry refuses.

---

diff --git a/iokit/Kernel/IOCPU.cpp b/iokit/Kernel/IOCPU.cpp
--- a/iokit/Kernel/IOCPU.cpp
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/IOCPU.cpp
@@ -590,6 +590,21 @@ IOCPUInterruptController::initCPUInterruptController(int sources, int cpus)
 void
 IOCPUInterruptController::registerCPUInterruptController(void)
 {
+	/* nio: nothing in this tree attaches this controller, so it has no
+	 * parent in the service plane and registerService() throws it away as
+	 * a non member.  Link it into the service plane under the registry
+	 * root first -- registry entry only, deliberately: attach() to the
+	 * platform expert would also give it a provider, and busy propagation
+	 * then walks that edge into an arbitration lock the matching thread
+	 * already holds, which is the cycle that prints SM rec c=2 for ever.
+	 * registerService() only needs the chain to reach the root. */
+	if (getProvider() == NULL) {
+		if (attachToParent(getRegistryRoot(), gIOServicePlane)) {
+			IOLog("nio: AT cpuic\n");
+		} else {
+			IOLog("nio: AT cpuic fail\n");
+		}
+	}
 	setProperty(gPlatformInterruptControllerName, kOSBooleanTrue);
 	registerService();
 
